Phil wrote:

>If you know that your dump will fit on the remote tape, then I suggest
>discarding "rdump" altogether and using a "dump | rsh dd" pipe.
>For example, if the tape drive is on machine "sun" and you are
>dumping from machine "du", log into "du" as root and run this command.
>This requires that you have the correct .rhosts file for root on both
>machines (which I think you need for rdump, too, anyway).
>
>	dump 0uf - | rsh sun dd obs=10k of=/dev/tape_device
>
>10k is the standard block size for dump tapes.  You could try 
>increasing that, say to 60k, for better tape performance.   But
>then you have to remember to provide a block size option when
>you try to restore from the tape.
>
>Years ago, I did some timed tests of rdump versus the dump | rsh dd
>pipeline on 4.3BSD UNIX systems.  The dump | rsh pipeline was nearly
>three times as fast!  Of course, with modern faster computers
>and perhaps improved software that may no longer be true.  In any
>case, I am sure that the dump | rsh pipeline is not any slower 
>than rdump.
>
>-Phil Farrell, Computer Systems Manager
>Stanford University School of Earth Sciences
>farrell@pangea.stanford.edu


