While I am not absolutely sure of this, I believe chfn operates only on the
/etc/passwd file, not on the NIS database.  In fact, I'm not even sure that
there is a place in the NIS database for the "finger" information.  There is
a special "yppasswd" utility for changing the password in the NIS database.
I can find no evidence of a "ypchfn" that would change the "finger" data.
So if you use chfn on a system other than the NIS master, and there is not
any record in the local /etc/passwd file for that user, I suspect that the
chfn will fail in exactly the manner you indicate -- from it's perspective
the user is non-existent.

I believe there is a way to put the user data in the local passwd file and
still use NIS to serve the password data, but I'd have to go review the man
page for the NIS and passwd stuff to confirm the syntax.

