Whitney Latta [latta@decatl.alf.dec.com]
Biggerstaff, Craig T=20
[Craig.T.Biggerstaff@USAHQ.UnitedSpaceAlliance.com]


Lars and Jie came up with the solution:
System V UNIX sets the group as of the process. BSD (Digital Unix)=20
does not. It is tunable with the variable sys_v_mode (you can set it=20
to 1). But you should preferably consult System V Compatibility User=B4s =

Guide to see the other things that will change when you set this.  See=20
open(2), mknod(2) and mkdir (2).
It can be changed by adding
sys_v_mode        1
to your /usr/sys/conf/<SYSTEM> file and rebuild the kernel.

++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++

Original Post:

Hello all,

This is probably a simple one.
/tmp under Solaris is setup by default drwxrwxrwt
Any file written to this directory has the permissions according to=20
the umask and the user as the owner and the users primary group id as=20
the group.
On digital unix it is the same only the group of the file created is=20
NOT the users primary group (which I'd prefer it to be) - rather it is=20
the group of whatever the parent directory is set to - ie if /tmp has=20
group sys - all files in the /tmp are set to group sys unless you=20
explicitly do a chgrp on them.
Anyone know why this happens and if there is a fix?
TIA
Sue
